To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array, we can use a modified version of the quickselect algorithm. Quickselect is a selection algorithm that can be used to find the kth smallest (or largest) element in an unordered list. In this case, we want to find the second largest element, so we will modify the algorithm to stop when we reach the second position.
Here's an implementation of the function:
```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    // Create a pivot index such that all elements smaller than the pivot
    // are on its left side and all elements greater than the pivot are on its right side
    int pivot = partition(arr, 0, n-1);

    // If the pivot index is equal to n-2, then return arr[n-1] as it is the second largest element
    if (pivot == n-2)
        return arr[pivot];

    // Otherwise, recursively call the function on either side of the pivot
    else if (pivot > n-2)
        return findSecondLargest(arr, pivot+1);
    else
        return findSecondLargest(arr, 0);
}
```
The `partition` function is used to rearrange the elements of the array so that all elements smaller than the pivot are on its left side and all elements greater than the pivot are on its right side. This allows us to determine which side of the pivot contains the second largest element.
Finally, the function returns the second largest element by calling itself recursively on either side of the pivot until the desired result is reached. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), making it efficient for large arrays.

